  "prose": [
    {
      "title": "Columns span",
      "text": "`Grid.Col` `span` prop controls the ratio of column width to the total width of the row.\nBy default, grid uses 12 columns layout, so `span` prop can be any number from 1 to 12.\n\nExamples:\n\n- `<Grid.Col span={3} />` – 3 / 12 = 25% of row width\n- `<Grid.Col span={4} />` – 4 / 12 = 33% of row width\n- `<Grid.Col span={6} />` – 6 / 12 = 50% of row width\n- `<Grid.Col span={12} />` – 12 / 12 = 100% of row width\n\n`span` prop also supports object syntax to change column width based on viewport width,\nit accepts `xs`, `sm`, `md`, `lg` and `xl` keys and values from 1 to 12. The syntax\nis the same as in [style props](/styles/style-props).\n\nIn the following example `span={{ base: 12, md: 6, lg: 3 }}`:\n\n- `base` – 12 / 12 = 100% of row width when viewport width is less than `md` breakpoint\n- `md` – 6 / 12 = 50% of row width when viewport width is between `md` and `lg` breakpoints\n- `lg` – 3 / 12 = 25% of row width when viewport width is greater than `lg` breakpoint",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Gutter",
      "text": "Set `gutter` prop to control spacing between columns. The prop works the same\nway as [style props](/styles/style-props) – you can reference `theme.spacing` values\nwith `xs`, `sm`, `md`, `lg` and `xl` strings and use object syntax to change gutter\nbased on viewport width:",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Grow",
      "text": "If `grow` prop is set, column will grow to fill the remaining space in the row:",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Column offset",
      "text": "Set `offset` prop on `Grid.Col` component to add gaps to the grid. `offset` prop\nsupports the same syntax as `span` prop: a number from 1 to 12 or an object with `xs`, `sm`, `md`, `lg` and `xl` keys and values from 1 to 12.",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Order",
      "text": "Set the `order` prop on `Grid.Col` component to change the order of columns. `order` prop\nsupports the same syntax as `span` prop: a number from 1 to 12 or an object with `xs`, `sm`, `md`, `lg` and `xl` keys and values from 1 to 12.",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Multiple rows",
      "text": "Once columns `span` and `offset` sum exceeds `columns` prop (12 by default),\ncolumns are moved to the next row:",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Justify and align",
      "text": "You can control `justify-content` and `align-items` CSS properties with `justify` and `align` props on `Grid` component:",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Auto sized columns",
      "text": "All columns in a row with `span=\"auto\"` grow as much as they can to fill the row.\nIn the following example, the second column takes up 50% of the row while the other two columns automatically resize to fill the remaining space:",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Fit column content",
      "text": "If you set `span=\"content\"`, the column's size will automatically adjust to match the width of its content:",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Change columns count",
      "text": "By default, grid uses 12 columns layout, you can change it by setting `columns` prop on `Grid` component.\nNote that in this case, columns span and offset will be calculated relative to this value.\n\nIn the following example, first column takes 50% with 12 span (12/24), second and third take 25% (6/24):",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "Container queries",
      "text": "To use [container queries](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_containment/Container_queries)\ninstead of media queries, set `type=\"container\"`. With container queries, all responsive values\nare adjusted based on the container width, not the viewport width.\n\nNote that, when using container queries, it is also required to set `breakpoints` prop\nto the exact container width values.\n\nTo see how the grid changes, resize the root element of the demo\nwith the resize handle located at the bottom right corner of the demo:",
      "format": "md"
    },
    {
      "title": "overflow: hidden",
      "text": "By default, `Grid` has `overflow: visible` style on the root element. In some cases\nyou might want to change it to `overflow: hidden` to prevent negative margins from\noverflowing the grid container. For example, if you use `Grid` without parent container\nwhich has padding.",
      "format": "md"
    }
  ],
